A spinlock using `std::atomic_flag` can cause CPU starvation because a thread repeatedly checks the lock in a tight loop instead of sleeping or yielding the CPU.

This is called busy-waiting. It can be especially problematic when a thread holds the lock for a long time or when a low-priority thread holds the
lock while a high-priority thread spins waiting for it.

## 1. Example: Spinlock using `atomic_flag`

```
#include <atomic>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>

class SpinLock {
    std::atomic_flag flag = ATOMIC_FLAG_INIT;

public:
    void lock() {
        while (flag.test_and_set(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
            // Busy-wait: repeatedly consumes CPU
        }
    }

    void unlock() {
        flag.clear(std::memory_order_release);
    }
};

SpinLock spinlock;
int shared_data = 0;

void worker(int id) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
        spinlock.lock();

        // Simulate a long critical section
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(
            std::chrono::milliseconds(2));

        ++shared_data;

        spinlock.unlock();
    }

    std::cout << "Worker " << id << " finished\n";
}

int main() {
    std::thread t1(worker, 1);
    std::thread t2(worker, 2);
    std::thread t3(worker, 3);
    std::thread t4(worker, 4);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();
    t3.join();
    t4.join();

    std::cout << "Final value: " << shared_data << '\n';
}
```

The example deliberately sleeps inside the critical section to simulate a long-held lock. Using a spinlock around a sleeping operation is a 
bad design because the waiting threads continue consuming CPU while the lock owner is asleep.

## 2. How CPU starvation happens

Thread T1 — lock owner

Acquires the lock and enters the critical section. It sleeps for 2 ms while still holding the lock.

Threads T2, T3 and T4 — waiting

Each repeatedly executes `flag.test_and_set()`. The calls return `true` while the lock is held, so all three threads keep spinning.

T1 releases the lock

One waiting thread may acquire it, but there is no fairness guarantee. The other threads resume spinning.

### What the CPU is doing

```
Time ──────────────────────────────────────────►

T1:  [ Acquire ][ Critical section ........ ][Unlock]
T2:  [ spin ][ spin ][ spin ][ spin ][ Acquire]
T3:  [ spin ][ spin ][ spin ][ spin ][ spin  ]
T4:  [ spin ][ spin ][ spin ][ spin ][ spin  ]

CPU:   Useful work by T1 + wasted cycles by waiters
```

Three important consequences:

1. CPU wastage: Waiting threads consume execution time without making progress.
2. Starvation: A thread may repeatedly lose the race to acquire the lock because `atomic_flag` does not guarantee FIFO fairness. Under sustained contention, it can wait indefinitely.
3. Priority inversion: On a priority-scheduled system, high-priority threads spinning can prevent a lower-priority lock owner from getting CPU time to release the lock. 
This can make the problem much worse.

Strictly speaking, busy-waiting alone does not guarantee starvation. Starvation depends on scheduling, contention and lock-acquisition fairness.

## 3. A better approach: reduce spinning

For a short critical section, a spinlock can be efficient. For longer waits, consider a mutex, which allows waiting threads to block rather than continuously consume CPU.

For C++20, you can also use `atomic_flag::wait()`:

```
#include <atomic>

class WaitableSpinLock {
    std::atomic_flag flag = ATOMIC_FLAG_INIT;

public:
    void lock() {
        while (flag.test_and_set(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
            flag.wait(true, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        }
    }

    void unlock() {
        flag.clear(std::memory_order_release);
        flag.notify_one();
    }
};
```

This reduces wasteful polling by allowing a contending thread to wait until the flag changes. However, it still does not guarantee fairness, and 
it is not equivalent to a mutex with guaranteed scheduling behavior.

Interview takeaway: `atomic_flag` makes the lock operation atomic; it does not make waiting fair or CPU-efficient. Spinlocks are best for very short 
critical sections where the owner is expected to release the lock almost immediately.
